This is the long version of the joke.

tsed accepts the command language of the classic ed line editor, but its
buffer, parser, regular-expression engine, I/O adapters, and executable are
written in TypeScript for Bun.

The project targets POSIX platforms and is a research toy.  This tour presents
the implementation that exists.  It does not claim complete POSIX conformance.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
BEFORE YOU START
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Install Bun and build the editor from the repository root:

    bun install
    bun run build

The build creates dist/tsed and the dist/ed symlink.  The minimum usable Bun
version is not known.  Development currently uses Bun 1.3.14.

The examples in this tour are real.  Each TRANSCRIPT contains a SCRIPT followed
by its exact OUTPUT.  After building, bun test and bun run test:tour execute
those scripts against dist/ed.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
HOW THE EDITOR IS PUT TOGETHER
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Input passes through a small set of explicit boundaries:

    argv and signals
           |
           v
    stdin bytes -> line reader -> parser -> editor
                                           |   |
                                           |   +-> BRE matcher
                                           v
                                      line buffer
                                           |
                                           v
                              stdout, files, and POSIX sh

The process boundary lives in src/main.ts.

It parses -s, -p prompt, --, and one optional pathname.  It classifies standard
input as a terminal, regular file, or another stream, constructs the editor,
installs signal handlers, and turns the editor result into process exit status.

SIGINT interrupts a pending editor operation.  SIGQUIT is ignored.  SIGHUP asks
the editor to preserve a modified buffer in ed.hup, first in the current
directory and then under HOME.

Byte I/O lives in src/io.ts.

stdin_line_reader turns Bun's asynchronous standard-input stream into
newline-delimited byte arrays.  It keeps partial reads and can wake a pending
read when an interrupt arrives.

The same module reads and writes files, converts between complete files and
editor lines, and runs commands through POSIX sh -c.  File data remains byte
oriented at this boundary.

Command parsing lives in src/parser.ts.

The parser separates an input line into zero, one, or two address
specifications, a one-character command, and the remaining argument.

Address expressions stay structured until the editor resolves them against the
current buffer.  This matters for semicolon ranges: resolving the first address
changes the current line before the second address is resolved.

The command machine lives in src/editor.ts.

The editor owns the command loop and the state outside the text buffer:
pathname, previous regular expression, previous replacement, previous shell
command, prompt mode, help mode, undo state, and modified-buffer warnings.

It resolves default ranges, validates addresses, dispatches commands, reports
errors, and decides whether an error ends a regular-file command script.
Buffer mutations use snapshots.  A failed mutation is rolled back, while a
successful one becomes undoable.

Stable lines live in src/buffer.ts.

line_buffer stores the complete file in memory.  Each line has a stable numeric
identity, while its visible address is its current array position.  Marks refer
to identities, so moving or inserting neighboring lines does not silently
retarget a mark.

The buffer owns insertion, deletion, replacement, joining, moving, copying,
marks, current-line updates, modified state, and snapshots.  Undo swaps the
current snapshot with the previous one, so repeated u commands toggle between
two states.

Basic regular expressions live in src/bre.ts.

The local BRE engine parses patterns into a small tree and matches them against
the editor's byte-preserving string representation.  It supports literals,
dot, anchors, bracket expressions, ASCII POSIX character classes, star,
intervals, captured groups, back-references, and replacement expansion.

Substitution can replace one match, every match, or a numbered match.
Ampersand inserts the complete match.  Backslash followed by 1 through 9
inserts a captured group.

Shared contracts live in src/types.ts.  That file contains addresses,
snapshots, input and output adapters, and ed_error.

src/build.ts compiles the executable.  src/style_check.ts checks whitespace and
source line length.  Unit tests exercise the parser, buffer, BRE engine, and
editor.  This TOUR supplies the process-level documentation tests.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
HOW ADDRESSES WORK
------------------------------------------------------------------------

An address identifies a line:

    .           current line
    $           last line
    number      absolute line number
    'x          line carrying mark x
    /BRE/       next matching line, wrapping forward
    ?BRE?       previous matching line, wrapping backward
    + or -n     offset from an address or the current line

Two addresses form a range.  A comma leaves the current line alone while
resolving the second address.  A semicolon makes the first address current
first.  A leading comma means 1,$.  A leading semicolon means .;$.

An address with no command prints that line.  A completely empty input line is
currently a no-op.

TRANSCRIPT addresses, searches, marks, and ranges
BEGIN SCRIPT
./dist/ed -s <<'ED'
a
alpha
beta
gamma
delta
.
2ka
'a=
/gamma/p
?alpha?p
.,$n
Q
ED
END SCRIPT
BEGIN OUTPUT
2
gamma
alpha
1	alpha
2	beta
3	gamma
4	delta
END OUTPUT
END TRANSCRIPT

ka attaches mark a to line 2.  Searches wrap around the buffer.  Numbered
printing uses a tab between the address and text.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
CHANGING TEXT
------------------------------------------------------------------------

These commands perform the basic mutations:

    a           append input after the addressed line
    i           insert input before the addressed line
    c           replace the addressed range with input
    d           delete the addressed range
    j           join addressed lines without a separator
    m address   move the range after the destination
    t address   copy the range after the destination
    u           swap with the previous undo snapshot

The a, i, and c commands read lines until a line containing only a dot.  The
terminating dot is not inserted.

TRANSCRIPT editing, copying, moving, and undo
BEGIN SCRIPT
./dist/ed -s <<'ED'
a
one
two
three
.
2i
one-and-half
.
1c
ONE
.
3d
1,$p
2t$
1m$
1,$p
u
1,$p
Q
ED
END SCRIPT
BEGIN OUTPUT
ONE
one-and-half
three
one-and-half
three
one-and-half
ONE
ONE
one-and-half
three
one-and-half
END OUTPUT
END TRANSCRIPT

The first print shows the insert, change, and deletion.  2t$ copies the second
line to the end.  1m$ moves the first line there.  u restores the state
immediately before the move.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
MATCHING AND SUBSTITUTION
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The command s/BRE/replacement/flags substitutes in the addressed range.  An
empty BRE reuses the previous one.

The implemented flags and expansions include:

    g           replace every match
    number      replace one numbered occurrence
    p, n, l     print the last changed line in that format
    &           insert the complete match
    \1 ... \9   insert a captured group

TRANSCRIPT BRE matching and substitution
BEGIN SCRIPT
./dist/ed -s <<'ED'
a
item 12
item 345
note
.
g/^item [[:digit:]]\{2\}$/p
1,2s/\(item\) \([0-9]*\)/\1 #\2/p
1,$p
Q
ED
END SCRIPT
BEGIN OUTPUT
item 12
item #345
item #12
item #345
note
END OUTPUT
END TRANSCRIPT

The global command first selects the two-digit item.  The substitution then
uses two captures across lines 1 and 2.  Its p flag prints the final changed
line, and the last command shows the complete result.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUNNING COMMANDS GLOBALLY
------------------------------------------------------------------------

g/BRE/commands executes commands for matching lines.  v selects non-matching
lines.  If the command list is omitted, both default to p.

G and V are interactive forms.  They print each selected line and read a
command for it.  An empty command skips that line.  An ampersand repeats the
previous interactive command.

Global selection records stable line identities before execution.  A command
may delete or move selected lines without turning later selections into
unrelated lines.

TRANSCRIPT global and inverse-global commands
BEGIN SCRIPT
./dist/ed -s <<'ED'
a
apple
apricot
berry
.
g/^a/s/^/fruit: /
v/^fruit:/p
1,$p
Q
ED
END SCRIPT
BEGIN OUTPUT
berry
fruit: apple
fruit: apricot
berry
END OUTPUT
END TRANSCRIPT


------------------------------------------------------------------------
PRINTING LINES
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Four commands expose the buffer:

    p           print lines as stored
    n           print address, tab, and line
    l           print escaped and folded lines ending in $
    =           print the addressed line number

The p, n, and l suffixes can be attached to many commands that change the
current line.  They print after the command succeeds.

TRANSCRIPT plain, numbered, and list output
BEGIN SCRIPT
./dist/ed -s <<'ED'
a
plain
.
l
n
p
Q
ED
END SCRIPT
BEGIN OUTPUT
plain$
1	plain
plain
END OUTPUT
END TRANSCRIPT

List mode gives common control bytes names such as \t, escapes backslash and
dollar, emits other non-printing bytes as three-digit octal, and folds after
70 rendered characters.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
READING AND WRITING FILES
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The file commands are:

    e file      replace the buffer, protecting modifications
    E file      replace the buffer without that protection
    f file      set or print the remembered pathname
    r file      read a file after the addressed line
    w file      write the addressed range

An initial pathname behaves like a forced edit.  Without an explicit argument,
e, E, r, and w use the remembered pathname when possible.  Reading the first
named file can establish it.  Writing the complete buffer clears the modified
flag.  Writing a partial range does not.

Unless -s is active, successful reads, edits, and writes print their byte
count.

TRANSCRIPT reading, naming, and writing files
BEGIN SCRIPT
tour_root=$PWD
tour_tmp=$(mktemp -d)
tour_cleanup()
{
    unlink "$tour_tmp/base" "$tour_tmp/extra" "$tour_tmp/result" \
        2>/dev/null || :
    rmdir "$tour_tmp" 2>/dev/null || :
}
trap tour_cleanup EXIT HUP INT TERM
cd "$tour_tmp"
printf 'red\nblue\n' > base
printf 'green\n' > extra
"$tour_root/dist/ed" -s base <<'ED'
r extra
f result
w
Q
ED
cat result
END SCRIPT
BEGIN OUTPUT
result
red
blue
green
END OUTPUT
END TRANSCRIPT

The example starts with base, reads extra, changes the remembered pathname to
result, and writes the complete buffer.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
TALKING TO THE SHELL
------------------------------------------------------------------------

There are three shell boundaries:

    !command        run with inherited standard streams
    r !command      capture output and insert it as lines
    w !command      send addressed lines to standard input

In a standalone shell escape, an unescaped percent sign expands to the
remembered pathname.  A leading second exclamation mark repeats the previous
shell command with any following text appended.  Without -s, a completed
standalone escape prints an exclamation mark.

TRANSCRIPT shell escapes and shell-backed file commands
BEGIN SCRIPT
./dist/ed -s <<'ED'
r !printf 'shell line\n'
1p
1w !tr a-z A-Z
!printf 'outside\n'
Q
ED
END SCRIPT
BEGIN OUTPUT
shell line
SHELL LINE
outside
END OUTPUT
END TRANSCRIPT


------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROMPTS, ERRORS, AND QUITTING
------------------------------------------------------------------------

-p prompt enables a prompt.  P toggles it.  -s suppresses byte counts and
shell-completion markers, not ordinary command output.

TRANSCRIPT prompt toggling
BEGIN SCRIPT
./dist/ed -s -p '> ' <<'ED'
a
hello
.
p
P
p
Q
ED
END SCRIPT
BEGIN OUTPUT
> > hello
> hello
END OUTPUT
END TRANSCRIPT

Errors print a question mark.  The h command prints the most recent reason.
H toggles automatic reasons after future errors.

TRANSCRIPT help mode and failure status
BEGIN SCRIPT
set +e
tour_output=$(./dist/ed -s <<'ED'
H
a
only
.
2p
h
Q
ED
)
tour_status=$?
set -e
printf '%s\n' "$tour_output"
test "$tour_status" -eq 1
END SCRIPT
BEGIN OUTPUT
?
invalid address
invalid address
END OUTPUT
END TRANSCRIPT

q warns once instead of discarding a modified buffer.  Repeating q allows the
quit if no intervening different command clears the warning.  Q always quits.
End of input acts like q.

A recoverable modified-buffer warning does not by itself force failure status.
Other command errors make the eventual process status nonzero.  A
non-recoverable error ends input classified as a regular file.  Terminal and
other streaming input can continue accepting commands.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
ALL COMMANDS AT A GLANCE
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    a           append input
    c           change a range
    d           delete a range
    e, E        protected and forced edit
    f           print or set the pathname
    g, G        global and interactive global
    h, H        print or automatically display error reasons
    i           insert input
    j           join lines
    k           set a mark
    l           list escaped lines
    m           move a range
    n           print numbered lines
    p           print lines
    P           toggle the prompt
    q, Q        protected and unconditional quit
    r           read a file or command output
    s           substitute BRE matches
    t           copy a range
    u           undo or redo the latest mutation
    v, V        inverse global and interactive inverse global
    w           write a range to a file or command
    =           print an address
    !           run a shell command


------------------------------------------------------------------------
CURRENT LIMITATIONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The target is POSIX platforms with Bun.  There is no Windows support layer.

The complete buffer and undo snapshot live in memory rather than an ed-style
temporary backing file.

File writes truncate and replace their destination directly.  They are not an
atomic rename transaction.

Text and regular-expression behavior is byte-oriented.  Character classes are
ASCII definitions, not locale-sensitive multibyte collation.  Bracket
collating and equivalence forms accept one byte-like character; they do not
implement a locale database.

A final input line without a newline is accepted.  Writing reconstructs every
buffer line with a trailing newline.

Bun and the host operating system still determine behavior for signals not
explicitly handled by the entry point.

This tour intentionally contains no material, logs, commands, names, or result
summaries from private or restrictively licensed test suites.
